  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1255 AM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nPatchy to areas of fog have developed early this morning over the\ninland Coastal Plains and eastern Brush Country, occasionally\ndense. Therefore, a Dense Fog Advisory is now in effect through 8\nAM this morning for visibilities down to a quarter of a mile.\n\nThe forecast is continuing to remain on track. A surface low will \nlift out of the northern Gulf, pushing onshore near the Florida-\nGeorgia line. This will allow for the return of onshore allowing for \nmoisture pooling ahead of our next cold front. That front is \nexpected to sweep through the region Sunday, with a reinforcing \nsurge of high pressure behind it. This will allow for cooler \ntemperatures to remain balmy and mild through the end of next week. \nSome medium to high rain chances (40-80%) will exist along the front \nas it moves through South Texas Sunday. With the latest model runs, \na brief isolated risk for strong to severe storms will be possible \nthough confidence is minimal at this time. Low-level moisture \nconvergence and favorable forcing along the cold front will act as \nthe primary focus for convective initiation. Combined with a \nmoderate to very unstable air mass (CAPE of 1,200-2000 J/kg;Lifted \nindices of -4 to -7) and 0-6 km bulk shear around 45-50 kts supports \nstrong to severe storm development. The caveat will be the speed of \nthe front which could limit the time those storms have to develop in \nthe environment. Will continue to monitor this over the next few \nmodel runs. \n\nMinor coastal flooding will remain a threat through the weekend as \nhigher than normal tides and long period swells continue.",
